00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.855
Pinterest, Twitter, things of that nature

00:10:02.855 --> 00:10:04.769
and there I think you're
just doing your thing.

00:10:04.769 --> 00:10:06.547
I've seen a ton of people manage both.

00:10:06.547 --> 00:10:09.030
I'm a big fan of something
with brands here talk

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.700
called Spanglish, you know,
which is like Spanish English.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.395
I'm very intrigued by some
of the work we've done

00:10:14.395 --> 00:10:17.830
for Latino brands where we
start a sentence in a Tweet

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:19.642
in Spanish and then finish it in English.

00:10:19.642 --> 00:10:22.660
Have you tried the
German English play yet?

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.448
Where you actually are putting out content

00:10:24.448 --> 00:10:26.306
that has both languages in it.

00:10:26.306 --> 00:10:29.022
In the post and the copy
hack a little bit there.

00:10:29.022 --> 00:10:29.887
I think I just gave a lot of people

00:10:29.887 --> 00:10:30.963
a good little nugget there.

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:31.867
I think that will work.

00:10:31.867 --> 00:10:35.556
I think you'll see a real
over-indexing opportunity there,

00:10:35.556 --> 00:10:38.389
especially with the youth who
are playing in both languages

00:10:38.389 --> 00:10:41.477
and who grew up in
households where, like I did,

00:10:41.477 --> 00:10:42.499
with Russian and English.

00:10:42.499 --> 00:10:44.357
You start a sentence in Russian

00:10:44.357 --> 00:10:45.750
and you finish it in English.

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.886
That's how a bilingual works

00:10:47.886 --> 00:10:50.091
and I think you should play with that.

00:10:50.091 --> 00:10:51.973
